item.text:
The 2022 dossier is mixed: Tshisekedi’s government kept formal electoral competition alive and did some legal/administrative work around the 2023 election, but the year was also marked by contested control of electoral machinery, repeated protest suppression under the state of siege, and sustained pressure on journalists and opposition figures.

**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["4B.1"]}

**New evidence**
- 2022 election-law process was contested; the opposition boycotted the National Assembly debate because it wanted the CENI composition reviewed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC: débat général ce vendredi à l'Assemblée nationale sur la réforme de la loi électorale","publisher":"Actualite.cd","publication_date":"2022-04-22","url":"https://actualite.cd/2022/04/22/rdc-debat-general-ce-vendredi-lassemblee-nationale-sur-la-reforme-de-la-loi-electorale","claim":"The opposition boycotted the Assembly debate on electoral-law reform because it wanted first a consensus on reviewing CENI composition.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Congolese reporting on a concrete parliamentary process with named institutions and parties.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target year 2022; directly about the election-law environment Tshisekedi governed.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi's governing coalition and the institutional setup it oversaw, though the article reports opposition behavior rather than a presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["Boycott itself does not prove unilateral manipulation, only that the process lacked broad trust."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.10"]}

- Tshisekedi’s 2021 CENI reshuffle fed the inherited 2022 baseline: parliament confirmed Denis Kadima, and civil society said the move left Tshisekedi in control of the body.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC : l'Assemblée nationale entérine le rapport de la commission, Denis Kadima à la tête de la Ceni","publisher":"Actualite.cd","publication_date":"2021-10-16","url":"https://actualite.cd/2021/10/16/rdc-lassemblee-nationale-enterine-le-rapport-de-la-commission-denis-kadima-la-tete-de-la","claim":"Parliament confirmed Denis Kadima as CENI head after a process criticized by civil society as favoring Tshisekedi's control of the election body.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting on an institutionally important appointment with direct relevance to the next election cycle.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-2022 inherited baseline that shaped 2022 electoral competition.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed through Tshisekedi's role in the CENI reshuffle and the civil-society allegation that the body remained under his influence.","contrary_evidence":["This is an allegation from civil society, not a judicial finding of manipulation."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.4","4B.10"]}

- The UN Security Council briefing on DRC noted that the opposition did not take part in the National Assembly vote on electoral-law revisions because it wanted the CENI composition reviewed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo: Briefing and Consultations","publisher":"Security Council Report","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/whatsinblue/2022/06/democratic-republic-of-the-congo-briefing-and-consultations-6.php","claim":"The briefing notes that the opposition did not participate in the National Assembly vote on electoral-law revisions, citing the need to review CENI composition.","locator":"political situation section","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent UN-focused analysis summarizing the Secretary-General's report and the parliamentary process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to the election-law debate.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi because the governing majority controlled the process and the dispute centered on institutions he had reshaped.","contrary_evidence":["This is a secondary summary, not the original UN report text."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4"]}

- Amnesty said the state of siege in North Kivu and Ituri was being used to crush dissent, with arbitrary detentions, military-court prosecutions, and protests being suppressed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC: One year on, 'State of Siege' used as a tool to crush dissent","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/drc-one-year-on-state-of-siege/","claim":"The state of siege was used to silence critics through arbitrary detention, military-court prosecutions, and violent suppression of protest.","locator":"background and briefing summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed human-rights briefing with named incidents, interviews, and a clear description of the legal regime.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target year 2022, though the emergency regime began in 2021 and continued through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Tshisekedi because he declared and repeatedly extended the state of siege and his appointed military/police authorities enforced it.","contrary_evidence":["The stated rationale was civilian protection against armed groups, so the source is contesting effectiveness and legality rather than denying the security threat."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.5","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- Amnesty again warned in September that authorities were threatening to crush protests and that blanket bans and arrests under the state of siege were chilling civic space.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC: Stop using prolonged state of siege as excuse to crush protests","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/drc-stop-using-prolonged-state-of-siege-as-excuse-to-crush-protests/","claim":"Authorities threatened to crush protests and were using blanket bans and arrests under the state of siege to suppress accountability and dissent.","locator":"opening paragraphs and background","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights reporting focused on the same emergency regime and on named protest events.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi through the emergency regime and his appointed provincial and security authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The protests were tied to eastern insecurity and M23/Rwanda-related anger, so the underlying grievance was not invented by the government."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- Human Rights Watch said martial law in the east had suppressed peaceful critics, curtailed expression, and involved lethal force and arbitrary detention.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DR Congo: Martial Law Brings Crackdown in East","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/22/dr-congo-martial-law-brings-crackdown-east","claim":"Military authorities in North Kivu and Ituri curtailed expression, broke up peaceful demonstrations with lethal force, and arbitrarily detained and prosecuted activists, journalists, and opposition members.","locator":"opening paragraph and article body","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-documented rights report by a major independent monitor with concrete examples and direct attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target year 2022, with the emergency regime still in force.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed through Tshisekedi's declaration and maintenance of martial law and the conduct of authorities operating under his administration.","contrary_evidence":["The source is critical and does not assess every area of the country equally; it focuses on the east and on documented incidents."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- The U.S. State Department report says people could usually criticize the government, but public criticism sometimes led to intimidation or arrest.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089109.html","claim":"Public criticism of officials sometimes resulted in intimidation, threats, or arrest, especially in provinces under the state of siege.","locator":"Section 2.a, Freedom of Expression, Including for Members of the Press and Other Media","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official report with concrete country-year findings and broad institutional coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report covers 2022 conduct and conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Tshisekedi administration and provincial authorities acting under it; the report distinguishes this from purely private speech.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that people generally could criticize the government in private without official reprisals."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- The same U.S. State Department report also says the government did not restrict or disrupt internet access in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089109.html","claim":"The government did not restrict or disrupt internet access, though the report notes some monitoring of private online communications without clear legal authority.","locator":"Internet Freedom subsection","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official report with a narrow, concrete factual finding that is useful as favorable counterevidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to the national executive because it describes what Tshisekedi's government did and did not do on internet control.","contrary_evidence":["The same section notes some monitoring of private online communications without appropriate legal authority."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- RSF described an oppressive legal framework, local pressure on journalists, and weak media independence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"République démocratique du Congo 2022 - EN","publisher":"RSF","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://rsf.org/en/analyse_regionale/627","claim":"The legal framework was described as obsolete and oppressive, with local authorities often pressuring journalists and media outlets.","locator":"Political context and Legal framework sections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual press-freedom assessment from a specialist monitor with broad, country-level coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Annual 2022 assessment tied to the same year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Tshisekedi-era environment because it describes the media climate under his administration, though it does not isolate one order by him.","contrary_evidence":["This is a general media-environment assessment rather than a single incident or direct order."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- CPJ reported that police detained three journalists in Bumba for more than a week over an insult complaint.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC authorities detain 3 journalists for over a week in insult case","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/drc-authorities-detain-3-journalists-for-over-a-week-in-insult-case/","claim":"Police detained three Radio Mwana Mboka journalists in Bumba for more than a week after a broadcast criticized a public official.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist press-freedom monitor with named journalists, dates, and local sourcing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Tshisekedi administration only in the sense that the police and prosecution system operated under it; the immediate action was provincial police and prosecutors.","contrary_evidence":["This is a single local incident and does not by itself establish a nationwide policy."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- CPJ also reported that two journalists were held for five months after covering a protest in Mbandaka.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Two Congolese journalists detained for 5 months after covering a protest","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/two-congolese-journalists-detained-for-5-months-after-covering-a-protest/","claim":"Two journalists were held without charge for five months after covering an anti-governor protest in Mbandaka.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear, contemporaneous reporting of prolonged detention tied to protest coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The immediate actors were provincial authorities and police, but the case is relevant to Tshisekedi because it reflects the national tolerance environment for protest coverage.","contrary_evidence":["The report centers on provincial officials, so national-level direction is inferred rather than directly documented."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- In Kinshasa, CPJ said police arrested, detained, and beat three journalists covering an opposition protest.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC police detain, beat 3 journalists covering protest","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-09-02","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/09/drc-police-detain-beat-3-journalists-covering-protest/amp/","claim":"Police arrested, detained, and physically abused three journalists while they covered an opposition protest in Kinshasa.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist monitor with named victims, date, location, and direct reporting from the journalists.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi because national police acted under his administration and the protest concerned opposition politics.","contrary_evidence":["The report describes police conduct at one protest; it does not show a formal nationwide censorship order."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- CPJ said military intelligence agents arrested and beat a journalist in North Kivu over routine coverage.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC military detains, beats journalist Christophe Katembo Muthahinga","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/10/drc-military-detains-beats-journalist-christophe-katembo-muthahinga/","claim":"Four military intelligence agents arrested and beat journalist Christophe Katembo Muthahinga over traffic coverage in North Kivu.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct, contemporaneous press-freedom documentation with named perpetrators and victim.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the national military/intelligence chain under Tshisekedi's presidency, though the immediate perpetrators were lower-level officers.","contrary_evidence":["The incident was local and does not by itself prove a central order to target that journalist."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- Reuters reported that Tshisekedi's former ally Jean-Marc Kabund said Republican Guard members raided his house and that he resigned over bullying and humiliation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vice president of Congo's parliament resigns over bullying, humiliation","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-01-14","url":"https://www.fijitimes.com.fj/vice-president-of-congos-parliament-resigns-over-bullying-humiliation/","claim":"Republican Guard members raided Jean-Marc Kabund's house, and he resigned saying he had suffered bullying, humiliation, and torture.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a concrete intra-elite coercion incident with direct quotations and named security forces.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Tshisekedi's political environment because the Republican Guard is a presidential force and Kabund was a key political figure in the governing camp turned critic.","contrary_evidence":["The account is based on Kabund's description; the government and Guard declined comment in the report."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8","4B.9"]}

- Reuters later reported Kabund's arrest after he criticized Tshisekedi, showing pressure on a prominent opposition figure.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Congo arrests opposition leader Kabund","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/congo-arrests-opposition-leader-kabund-165127790.html","claim":"Opposition leader Jean-Marc Kabund was arrested after controversy over remarks criticizing President Felix Tshisekedi.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting on a politically salient arrest with named actor, date, and stated charge.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the national executive climate because the case centered on criticism of the president and was handled by state authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The report says the charge was not fully specified at the time, so the exact legal basis remained contested."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8"]}

- JED's 2022 annual tally found 124 press-freedom violations, including arrests, assaults, kidnappings, and one killing.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC : 124 cas d'atteintes à la liberté de la presse dont un décès et 2 enlèvements documentés en 2022 (Rapport JED)","publisher":"L'Interview.cd","publication_date":"2022-11-02","url":"https://linterview.cd/rdc-124-cas-datteintes-a-la-liberte-de-la-presse-dont-un-deces-et-2-enlevements-documentes-en-2022-rapport-jed/","claim":"JED recorded 124 press-freedom violations in 2022, including arrests, assaults, kidnappings, and one journalist death.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local-language reporting of a specialist watchdog's annual tally, with concrete counts and categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Tshisekedi because the violations occurred under his presidency, though many incidents were provincial rather than ordered from Kinshasa.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a report about violations, not an official government record or a court finding of central direction."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10","4B.3"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across Reuters mirrors, CPJ, Amnesty, HRW, RSF, the U.S. State Department report, Security Council Report, Actualite.cd, JED coverage, and civil-society election material.
- I rejected duplicate or redundant hits: multiple Amnesty English/French mirrors of the same state-of-siege reporting, multiple CPJ items covering similar harassment themes, and older background pieces that only repeated the same 2021-2022 baseline.
- I rejected out-of-period items, including later 2023-2024 election and constitutional-change coverage, except where they were only used as search noise and not retained.
- I did not use Freedom House, V-Dem, WGI, RSF structured country-year entries as evidence because the local prior already supplied those facts and the instructions explicitly forbid re-fetching those datasets for citation.
- I also rejected weaker, non-specific items that described general Congolese politics without clear 2022 ruler-period relevance or without a direct link to Tshisekedi's authority.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no strong target-period evidence that Tshisekedi materially strengthened independent courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or other oversight institutions in 2022.
- Evidence on political equality for women, minorities, and unpopular viewpoints is thin at the ruler-specific level for 2022.
- There is only limited direct evidence on surveillance or digital controls beyond the State Department's internet-freedom note.
- The record shows persistent abuse and pressure, but some incidents are provincial or military and not a direct presidential order; more evidence on command responsibility would sharpen attribution.
- More search could help on 4B.4 and 4B.7, but it is unlikely to overturn the overall mixed picture unless it surfaces a clear, ruler-attributed institutional reform or a documented transfer of power.
